graders.evidence:
Before I deploy, I need one thing from you, and there's one change your code needs.

**1. Your bot token.** The bot reads `TELEGRAM_TOKEN` from the environment, so I'll set it as an environment variable on the app. It won't go into the code, and Back4app's tools never show it again after it's set. Paste it here (it's the token @BotFather gave you, like `123456:ABC-...`). If you'd rather not share it in chat, say so. I'll deploy without it and you can add it in the Back4app dashboard after you claim the app.

**2. The bot needs a small web server.** Back4app checks that every app answers HTTP on the `PORT` environment variable. If it doesn't, the deployment fails. Your bot only polls Telegram, so I'll add a few lines that answer `200` on `/`, using Node's built-in `http` module so there are no new dependencies:

```js
const http = require('http');
const TelegramBot = require('node-telegram-bot-api');

const bot = new TelegramBot(process.env.TELEGRAM_TOKEN, { polling: true });
bot.on('message', (msg) => bot.sendMessage(msg.chat.id, msg.text));

http.createServer((req, res) => res.end('ok')).listen(process.env.PORT || 8080);
```

**How it will work:** you have no account, so I'll make a free temporary Back4app account. It lasts 24 hours. I'll give you a claim link, and opening it and signing in keeps the bot online. If you don't claim it, the account and app are deleted. I'll wait for your token before creating the account so none of the 24 hours goes to waiting.

Also, run the bot in only one place. If it's still polling on your own machine, Telegram will return conflict errors to both copies.

